## Step 4a: Apply the Super-Clear Fixes (this run)

Sort every finding into two lanes. The gate question for CLEAR: **would a reasonable person pick anything other than your recommendation?** If no, it is CLEAR, apply it, even if you framed it as options in your head. Do not defensively route an obvious fix to a fork just because you can imagine alternatives (over-forking clear fixes is the failure mode {{USER_NAME}} flagged, July 2026).
- **CLEAR** — apply directly this run. Covers both (1) mechanically-obvious fixes (typos, wrong tool/skill/file names, dead references, broken paths, stale facts with an authoritative source, single-canonical-answer contradictions) AND (2) issues where analysis lands on a single clearly-correct fix with no genuine competing resolution, i.e. the "options" collapse to "the right fix vs. leaving it broken." A fix that is safe regardless of an open question is CLEAR (e.g. dropping a volatile count sidesteps needing the exact number).
- **FORK** — report for {{USER_NAME}}, do NOT apply. Reserve for genuine decisions: two-or-more reasonable resolutions where {{USER_NAME}}'s preference actually matters (a real tradeoff, not fix-vs-broken); a change that hinges on a fact only {{USER_NAME}} knows or on an external verification you cannot sidestep; deleting load-bearing content; OR any change that moves/renames/deletes files or symlinks or edits an executable script/plist/config/code path (a wrong unattended edit there breaks a job) — these stay FORKs even when the recommended fix looks obvious. When verification of a would-be CLEAR fix is not clean, demote to FORK.

Discipline for the CLEAR lane:
- **Verify before you fix.** Re-read the actual file/line and confirm the issue is real and current. Reports go stale and repeat false positives (e.g. a past report claiming a tool in some skill doesn't exist when it does). If verification isn't clean, demote it to a FORK.
- **Edit at the source, never the rendered copy.** CLAUDE/IDENTITY/SOUL/AGENTS/TOOLS/SECURITY, `prompts/*.md`, generic `recurring/*.md`, and `launchd/*.plist` are rendered from `{{MINERU_HOME}}/engine/**/*.template` plus `{{MINERU_HOME}}/active/overrides/` and the `*.local.md` files; a direct edit is erased by the next install. Personal fixes go to the `.local.md` file or the override; generic fixes go to the engine template (that is a commit to the engine repo, which is headed for public: no personal content). After editing, re-render with `{{MINERU_HOME}}/engine/../.venv/bin/mineru profile install --target {{MINERU_HOME}} --apply`.
- Minimal, surgical edits in the file's own voice. Never touch secrets/credentials/PII. Preserve every safety guardrail (read-only paths stay read-only, the firewall stays intact). Never `rm` and never remove files or symlinks here (that's a FORK). Never use em-dashes.
- Persist the fixes: `.mineru` edits ride the daily backup. If you edited any `{{USER_CLAUDE_HOME}}/skills/` file, commit and push it via `/sync-claude-toolkit` (claude-toolkit is {{USER_NAME}}'s personal repo, pre-authorized to push, keep it current). No AI attribution in commit messages.
- Keep a changelog (file + one-line before→after) for the report.

